{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["<b>The name of YHWH is a tower of strength; the righteous runs into it and is set on high (Proverbs 18:10).</b>" "<b>Solomon,</b> king, peace be upon him, comes to teach us in this verse (Proverbs 18) the trait of trust (bitaḥon). The meaning of the verse is that whoever trusts in the name of YHWH is like a man who is in a tower of strength and sits there in safety and fears no person, as it is written (Psalms 118:6): ״YHWH is for me; I shall not fear — what can man do to me?״ 'The righteous runs into it and is set on high' — from trouble; as it is written (Proverbs 29:25): ״but whoever trusts in YHWH shall be set on high,״ that is, from every trouble and from an enemy. For if one ascends the highest mountains or hides in the lowest valleys, he is not exalted above the enemy; but the name of YHWH exalts him above him. And the word 'into it' does not refer back to the tower but to the name of YHWH, for a person does not run inside a tower of strength, but whoever runs in the name of YHWH is exalted as though in a tower. Therefore it says that the righteous will run in the name of YHWH and not stumble. And since the way of a runner is to stumble even on level ground, it says that the one who trusts is not so, for he shall run and not stumble, as it is written (Proverbs 4:12): ״and if you run, you shall not stumble.״ And the reason it says 'a tower of strength' and not 'the name of YHWH is a high mountain' is that a tower is a great necessity for people in time of trouble and in time of war, as it is written regarding Abimelech (Judges 9:51): ״and a tower of strength was in the midst of the city, and they fled there״ — therefore he likened the name of YHWH to a tower of strength, because the trust of creatures in the name of YHWH saves them from every trouble. And so it is written (Psalms 20:2): ״May YHWH answer you on the day of trouble; may the name of the Elohim of Jacob set you on high״ — and that is the strong tower in which creatures are exalted in the time of their trouble. And so David said (Psalms 18:3 — numbered as 29 by the commentary): ״from my strivings You set me on high,״ and so he said to Goliath (1 Samuel 17:45): ״You come to me with sword and spear and javelin, but I come to you in the name of YHWH of hosts, the Elohim of the ranks of Israel.״ And just as Solomon, peace be upon him, likened the name of YHWH to a tower, so Isaiah, peace be upon him, likened it to a chamber where a person shuts the door behind him and is exalted in it, as it is written (Isaiah 26:20): ״Go, my people, enter your chambers and shut your door behind you.״ And we find in verses that He, may He be exalted, is likened in every place to a dwelling, a chamber, a tower, a rock: a dwelling — (Psalms 90:1) ״YHWH, You have been a dwelling for us״; a chamber — ״Go, my people, enter your chambers״; a tower — ״The name of YHWH is a tower of strength״; a rock — (2 Samuel 22:2) ״YHWH is my rock and my fortress.״ And because He, may He be exalted, is in every place, therefore He is likened in every place. And our Sages, of blessed memory, called His name 'Place' (Makom), because He, may He be exalted, encompasses place and all its parts, and He is the place of the world. And you should know that a person is not called righteous unless he trusts in Him, as it is written (Proverbs 28:1): ״and the righteous are as bold as a lion״ — for each one of them trusts in the name of the Most High like a lion that trusts in its strength. And the verse teaches you that the righteous man runs in his trust to exert himself in commandments and to serve the Master of the universe night and day and to labor for the honor of His name and not grow weary, for his intellective soul strengthens him and adds power within him. And so the prophet said (Isaiah 40:31): ״but those who hope in YHWH shall renew strength; they shall mount up with wings as eagles; they shall run and not grow weary; they shall walk and not faint״ — the meaning of this is that they shall exert great effort and not grow weary, for there are commandments that require great effort and excessive toil, such as redeeming captives and saving lives, which are commandments requiring great alacrity; and concerning this it says, 'they shall run and not grow weary,' meaning that the righteous exchange strength and might in divine service and in commandments, and therefore they are not wearied. And this is what is written (Judges 5:31): ״and His lovers as the sun going forth in its might״ — he likened them to the sun in its might, which does not grow weary. And likewise there are commandments that require diligence and persistence, such as Torah study and acquiring the traits of fear; a person must accustom himself in them until the habit in them returns to him as nature. And concerning this it says, 'they shall walk and not faint' — and the language of ילכו is from the language of (Genesis 17:1) ״walk before Me,״ whose Aramaic translation is 'walk in My fear'; and the intent is that they exert themselves in the trait of fear and it does not return to them as exhaustion. And know that from the trait of fear a person arrives at the trait of trust, as it is written (Psalms 115:11): ״You who fear YHWH, trust in YHWH,״ and therefore Solomon, peace be upon him, says here: 'The name of YHWH is a tower of strength; the righteous runs into it and is set on high' — explaining that the name of YHWH is a tower of strength for the righteous man who fears, and he is the one able to run, and trusts that he will not stumble in his course. And these two traits we find in Phinehas: he feared YHWH and trusted in Him in order to sanctify His name, as it is written (Malachi 2:5): ״My covenant was with him, life and peace; and I gave them to him for fear, and he feared Me, and because of My name he was humbled.״ It explains that He gave him these two traits — life and peace — as reward for the two traits that were in him: fear and trust. Fear — this is 'and I gave them to him for fear,' as reward for the fear that he feared Me. Trust — this is 'because of My name he was humbled': he trusted in the name of the Most High to come against the entire tribe to slay their prince and the king's daughter, and he was close to being killed, and was humbled before My name. Therefore he merited and was privileged in performing this deed; he was zealous for the great name and turned back the wrath — this is what is written." "<b>Phinehas son of Eleazar son of Aaron the priest, etc. He turned back My wrath.</b> Some commentators explained: he caused it to rest — from the language of (Isaiah 30:15) בשובה ונחת ('in returning and rest'); for through his zealotry he caused the attribute of justice to rest, in the manner of (Exodus 33:14) והניחותי לך ('and I will give you rest'). But what is understood from the word השיב is that the wrath was fit to go forth against Israel, but Phinehas turned it back entirely and it did not go." "<b>And one may ask:</b> how did it not go forth? — and there died of Israel twenty-four thousand! But this is a proof that all of them were from the tribe of Simeon, and not one of Israel from the remaining tribes was stricken. And this is the turning back of the wrath from the rest of Israel; and this is the meaning of 'He turned back My wrath from the children of Israel' — that the attribute of justice did not prevail outside the tribe. This is 'and I did not consume the children of Israel in My zealotry' — I did not consume a single one of Israel. You thus learn that the turning back of the wrath caused the continued existence of Israel, for had it not been so, they would all have been consumed. And if so, Phinehas caused Israel long life and years of life from that time until the end of the world; and those years have no end or limit. Therefore he merited to live without end — measure for measure. For this Phinehas is Elijah, as the prophet said (Malachi 2:5): ״My covenant was with him, life and peace,״ and it is said here: 'My covenant of peace.'" "<b>In being zealous for My zealotry.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): he rose in My place, for it is written of the Holy One, blessed be He (Exodus 20): אל קנא ('a zealous El')." "<b>And by way of the Midrash.</b> בקנאו את קנאתי — 'when he avenged My vengeance' — two acts of vengeance: one here and one that he is destined to enact in the days of Ahab. And so it is written there (I Kings 19:9-10): ״ויהי דבר ה׳ אליו ויאמר לו מה לך פה אליהו״ — 'and the word of HaShem came to him and He said to him, what are you doing here, Elijah' — ״ויאמר קנא קנאתי לה׳ אלהי צבאות כי עזבו בריתך בני ישראל״ — 'and he said, I have been very jealous for HaShem, the God of hosts, for the children of Israel have forsaken Your covenant.' Two acts of vengeance: one in the days of Moses and one in the days of Ahab. And so it is written in Judges, in the days of the concubine in Gibeah: ופינחס בן אלעזר נגיד היה עליהם לפנים וה׳ עמו — 'and Phinehas the son of Eleazar was ruler over them in times past, and HaShem was with him.'" "<b>And know</b> the opinion of our Rabbis, of blessed memory, that this Phinehas is Elijah. We learn it from what they said in Bava Metzia: Rabbah bar Avuha found Elijah standing in a gentile cemetery. He said to him: לאו כהנא את — 'are you not a priest?' — that is, he was astonished at him because he was not being careful about the impurity of a tent (טומאת אהל). And he said to him that gentiles do not impart impurity through a tent, but only Israelites do, as it is said (Numbers 19:14): ״אדם כי ימות באהל״ — 'a man, when he dies in a tent' — and it is written (Ezekiel 34:31): ״ואתן צאני צאן מרעיתי אדם אתם״ — 'and you My sheep, the sheep of My pasture, you are אדם (man).' Therefore we find that he stretched himself upon the son of the Zarephath woman who had died, because he was the son of a gentile woman."], :book :numbers, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :register {:flags {5 ["God"]}}, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:36:33.846932941Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}